
Run ./tools/test-firmware.sh from the repository root (or by absolute path).
Requires a C++17 compiler with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer;
set CXX to select a compiler. This is a host runner, not `pio test`.

The same regression suite runs for both machines, with flowmeters enabled and
disabled. It exercises real button handlers, state handlers, persistence helpers
and output actors. Only Arduino time/GPIO/Serial and the external Preferences
API are replaced. The fake clock wraps at 32 bits like millis() on ESP32.

Coverage includes programming isolation/cancellation, stop precedence, NVS open
and write failure, successful reload, timeout boundaries, clock rollover,
backflush without flow, and the boiler fault acknowledgement interlock.

The focused state-machine suites additionally exercise every dose button on both
groups, programming ownership and save failures, boiler probe sampling and
overfill transitions, tea start/stop/learning, group-specific fault acknowledgement,
simultaneous programming selection, and all 16 valve/pump state combinations.
Assertions use public handler methods, State, saved settings and GPIO outputs;
they do not access private handler state.

Button tests cover one-shot Press/Held events (Held exactly on sample 40, not
sample 41), release/reset, all dose buttons, every two-dose priority pair,
both backflush chord orders, and STOP/held-STOP precedence on both groups.

Input/output tests close simulated physical panel contacts and run the real
InputHandler, machine-specific matrix scanner, StateHandler and StateActor.
GPIO reads depend on the actual driven scan rows, rather than injecting decoded
events or matrix state. Both machine pinouts and output polarities are checked.
Scenarios include doses, tea (including ignored tea Held), STOP/backflush,
fresh GPIO press-and-hold fault acknowledgement, programming and 24 consecutive
mixed sessions on the same machine. S27 has only one physical group panel;
Cimbali tests both. Without flowmeters, GPIO-started doses are stopped manually.
The input decoder has process lifetime like the real firmware; keys are released
between scenarios, without resetting its private edge counters.

Additional state tests cross millis() rollover inside both backflush phases,
normal tea and tea learning, checking before/at/after rollover and exact duration
boundaries. Pulse tests cover immediately before/after extraction start, the
final pulse alongside STOP/learning save, and idle pulses before the next dose.
Tea/backflush timestamp arithmetic explicitly uses uint32_t to retain ESP32's
32-bit rollover behavior on hosts where unsigned long is 64 bits.

Run ./tools/test-firmware.sh --coverage for instrumented tests and a strict 100%
line, function, region and reachable-branch gate, separately for each of the four
machine/flowmeter variants. Requires Clang, matching llvm-cov/llvm-profdata and
Python 3 in addition to the sanitizers. On macOS the LLVM tools are found through
xcrun; elsewhere they must be on PATH. CXX, LLVM_COV and LLVM_PROFDATA can select
a matching toolchain.

The measured scope is BoilerStateHandler.h, GroupHeadHandler.h, StateHandler.h,
TeaStateHandler.h and StateActor.h. State.h only defines data and
GenericStateHandler.h only defines an abstract interface, so neither contains
executable behavior. Input decoding, persistence helpers, device drivers and
Arduino setup/loop are not included in the state-machine coverage percentage.
Raw LLVM reports are not filtered or rewritten. LLVM counts one unreachable
implicit default branch for the exhaustive private BoilerState switch: public
transitions can only assign its three declared enum values. The gate explicitly
prints this one uncovered outcome as UNREACHABLE and tolerates only that switch's
implicit default, not its cases or any other branch. LLVM 18 and newer LLVM
versions export its executed/unexecuted counters in opposite orders; both are
accepted only at this switch location. Consequently raw branch coverage is below
100% even though every reachable branch is covered. No private-state injection
or production-code changes are used to manufacture a higher percentage.

The gate is tools/check-state-coverage.py. Its CLI regression tests run
automatically before instrumented firmware tests (or separately with
python3 test/coverage-gate.py). They cover both LLVM counter orders and ensure
that additional uncovered branches, an unexecuted switch, incomplete coverage
and missing headers still fail.

HTML reports, summary.txt and coverage.json are written per variant under
.amp/in/artifacts/state-coverage/ (override with COVERAGE_DIR). Add /.amp/in/ to
the repository-local .git/info/exclude before using this default output path.
Any missing measured header or uncovered reachable line/branch/function/region fails the
command. Existing regression tests run alongside the focused suites in both
normal and coverage modes, linked into one binary per variant to avoid profile
collisions between separate test entry points.

GitHub Actions runs both commands on pushes, pull requests and manual dispatches
via .github/workflows/firmware-tests.yml. It uses Ubuntu 24.04 with matching
Clang/LLVM 18 tools, and sanitizer errors fail the job. Coverage reports for all
four variants are uploaded as the state-machine-coverage artifact; reports
already generated before a failure are uploaded as well. The same documented
unreachable BoilerState default exception applies to the CI coverage gate.

These tests do not verify electrical behavior, actual NVS, ISR scheduling or boot
levels. Build both targets with ./tools/build-firmware.sh and test the assembled
hardware separately before operating real loads. See docs/firmware.md.
